        <p>"Don’t be sorry. What's going on?”</p>
        <p>Betsy let out a nervous breath. "It's just them. My moms. It's just awful in there. I can't breathe. My mom Becky... she's just so sad. She cries to me, telling me that mom hates her now because she's broken. She thinks she wants to leave us."</p>
        <p>Carla felt anger, but she kept her face perfectly neutral.</p>
        <p>"And when I asked my Mom about it just now," Betsy continued, her voice cracking, "she just looked so tired. She promised she wasn't leaving, but she just seems so quiet. So far away."</p>
        <p>"It's hard to watch two people you love go through a crisis," Carla said carefully. She couldn't bash Becky directly, or Betsy would close up. "Accidents like that... they change the whole dynamic of a house. Your mom is in a lot of pain, so she's scared. And Lisa is a cop. She's used to being able to fix things, and she can't fix a broken bone. Taking care of someone around the clock takes a massive toll."</p>
        <p>The kettle went off. Carla turned, dropping two tea bags into the mugs and pouring the boiling water over them. She slid one mug across to Betsy.</p>
        <p>Betsy wrapped her cold hands around the warm mug, letting the heat soak into her skin. She looked at Carla.</p>
        <p>"But it isn't just the stress," Betsy said. "My mom Becky... she's acting weird. Not just sad. Weird."</p>
        <p>Carla went still. She took a slow sip of her tea, then asked. "Weird how?</p>
        <p>Betsy shifted uncomfortably on the stool, looking down into her tea. "Like she's playing a game. Yesterday, mom ran out to get the mail, and I was in the kitchen. I walked down the hall to ask Mom if she wanted the TV on. The door was cracked."</p>
        <p>Betsy paused, looking guilty for even saying it out loud.</p>
        <p>"What did you see, Betsy?" Carla asked, keeping her voice gentle, recognizing exactly how important this moment was.</p>
        <p>"She was reaching for a book," Betsy whispered. "On very top shelf of the bookcase. She was using her bad arm. The one with the broken collarbone in the sling. She stood and she reached right up, pulled it down, and set it on her lap. She didn't look like she was in pain at all."</p>
        <p>Carla’s eyes were wide.</p>
